C = [
("h1", "5. Offence and Gift"),
("p", "Claim: Romans 5:15–17 is a study in proportion. Paul sets an offence beside a gift three times, and each time the gift is the greater. The verses give two things the later verses depend on: the gift is not a mere reversal of the harm, and it reaches those who receive it. This chapter reads the three verses one at a time and then sets the pairs in a table."),
("sub", "5.1 Verse 15: the offence and the gift"),
("p", "“But not as the offence, so also is the free gift. For if through the offence of one many be dead, much more the grace of God, and the gift by grace, which is by one man, Jesus Christ, hath abounded unto many” (Romans 5:15). The argument has the form of “if…much more.” If the offence of one was enough to bring death to many, how much more is the grace of God, given by one man, enough to bring abundance to many. Paul argues from the lesser to the greater. The offence is the lesser fact, and it is known. The gift is the greater, and it is shown by being set against it."),
("p", "Two words are paired: the offence and the free gift. The offence is a falling aside, a stepping away from a path. The free gift is a thing given out of grace, without payment. Paul says that the gift is “by grace” and that it is “by one man, Jesus Christ.” Grace is the source of the gift, and Christ is its channel. The sentence also repeats the word “many”: “many be dead,” “hath abounded unto many.” The volume reads “many” here as Paul wrote it. It is a word of number, and it does not say, in this verse, which of the many it is that the gift reaches."),
("sub", "5.2 Verse 16: the judgment and the free gift"),
("p", "“And not as it was by one that sinned, so is the gift: for the judgment was by one to condemnation, but the free gift is of many offences unto justification” (Romans 5:16). The second correction is one of kind. The judgment came from a single offence and ended in condemnation. The free gift came out of many offences and ended in justification. It is not the same transaction run the other way. One offence was enough to bring the judgment. The gift had to answer many offences, and it did."),
("p", "The two end-words are “condemnation” and “justification.” They are legal words. The first is a sentence passed; the second is a declaration of righteousness. They are the two words with which the argument of Romans 1:18 to 4:25 has already been dealing, and Paul uses them here to name the two results of the two acts. Nothing in the verse speaks of an inward change. It speaks of a standing: condemned, or declared righteous."),
("sub", "5.3 Verse 17: the reign"),
("p", "“For if by one man’s offence death reigned by one; much more they which receive abundance of grace and of the gift of righteousness shall reign in life by one, Jesus Christ” (Romans 5:17). Death reigned, as it did in verse 14. Now the other half: those who receive shall reign in life. The outcome of the second man’s act is greater than a cancelling of the harm. Death had a reign. They who receive are to have one, and it is a reign “in life.” The balance is not an even one. It tips on the side of the gift."),
("p", "Two details of the verse deserve to be marked. The first is the tense. “Death reigned” is past. “Shall reign in life” is future. Paul does not say in this verse when the reign begins, or where it is exercised. He says that those who receive shall reign. The second is the object of the receiving. The things received are “abundance of grace” and “the gift of righteousness.” Righteousness is called a gift. It is not wages, and it is not an achievement. The sentence agrees with Paul’s statement in chapter 4 that righteousness is counted to the man who “worketh not, but believeth” (Romans 4:5), and with the “free gift” of verses 15 and 16."),
("tbl", "The pairs in Romans 5:15–17.", [
    ("Verse", "KJV text", "What to observe"),
    ("Rom 5:15", "“through the offence of one many be dead”", "One offence; many dead"),
    ("Rom 5:15", "“the grace of God, and the gift by grace, which is by one man, Jesus Christ, hath abounded unto many”", "Grace and gift; abounding"),
    ("Rom 5:16", "“the judgment was by one to condemnation”", "One offence; judgment; condemnation"),
    ("Rom 5:16", "“the free gift is of many offences unto justification”", "The gift answers many; justification"),
    ("Rom 5:17", "“by one man’s offence death reigned by one”", "Death reigned"),
    ("Rom 5:17", "“they which receive abundance of grace and of the gift of righteousness shall reign in life by one, Jesus Christ”", "Those who receive; reign in life"),
  ], [0.14, 0.52, 0.34]),
("sub", "5.4 What the three verses establish"),
("p", "Three things are established. The first is that the gift is greater than the harm. Paul says it three times, with “much more” in verses 15 and 17 and with the contrast of one and many in verse 16. The second is that the gift is a gift: of grace, of righteousness, free. The third is that the second headship has a clause of reception that the first does not. Death reigned by one man’s offence, and nothing is said of the many doing anything to come under it. Life reigns in “they which receive.”"),
("p", "The third point is the one that the next chapter must keep in view. When Paul comes to say, in verse 18, that the free gift came upon “all men,” the reader has already been told in verse 17 who it is that has the gift: the ones who receive. The volume does not set verse 18 against verse 17. It reads verse 18 with verse 17 in the same parenthesis, as part of the one argument."),
("p", "A caution may be added. The word “receive” does not tell the reader what is to be received in the sense of the content of faith, or how, or when. Those are matters Paul treats elsewhere in the letter, and the volume does not treat them here. Its claim is smaller: in the text of Romans 5:17, the second headship is described by a clause of reception, and the first is not."),
]
